add('structure','5b3–10 completes the projecting-sidepost discussion from5a15–5b2.5b11–6a10 introduces side/front breaches and the bent-alley comparison.6a11 opens a baraita whose literal public-domain framing is corrected at6b4; do not close its source unit at core end.','5b:3','5b:10','5b:11','6a:10','6a:11','6b:4')
add('answer_position','Rav Pappa answers placement of the additional sidepost: put it on the opposite side. This answers the concern that placing it beside the projection merely adds to it.','5b:2','5b:3')
add('alternative_position','Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua alternatively permits placement beside it if the new feature is made larger or smaller so it is differentiated. Preserve both size alternatives without inventing an unprinted measurement axis.','5b:3')
add('conditional_position','Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua limits the earlier additional-sidepost requirement to an eight-amah alley.','5b:4')
add('conditional_position','In a seven-amah alley it is permitted through standing material exceeding the breach. Do not drop the seven/eight distinction or transfer this view to Rav Huna without patronymic.','5b:4')
add('support','The a-fortiori compares a courtyard, which is not permitted by lehi/beam yet is permitted by standing exceeding breach, to an alley permitted by lehi/beam.','5b:5')
add('objection','The courtyard comparison is challenged because its breach threshold is ten, whereas the alley is assumed to have a four-unit breach threshold. These are stages of an argument, not an undisputed final contrast.','5b:6')
add('answer','The answer says Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua holds the alley breach threshold also ten.','5b:7')
add('objection','The Gemara objects that the discussion was addressing Rav Huna, who holds a four-unit threshold. Distinguish the two named authorities.','5b:7')
add('resolution','Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua is expressing his own reason/view, not claiming the argument follows Rav Huna’s own premise.','5b:8')
add('alternative_position','Rav Ashi says even in an eight-amah alley no extra lehi is needed, reasoning by cases.','5b:9')
add('case','If standing material exceeds the opening, the alley is permitted on that basis.','5b:9')
add('case','If the opening exceeds the standing projection, the projection is treated as a lehi. Preserve the geometric case rather than deriving a universal sufficiency rule.','5b:9')
add('question_answer','If the two are exactly equal, the text treats it as doubt in a rabbinic matter and rules leniently. Preserve the doubtful-rabbinic rationale and do not silently turn the equality problem into a certain majority.','5b:10')
add('transmission','Rav Hanin bar Rava reports Rav’s breach rule, continued across the folio. Raw5b11 prints חנין while subsequent discussion prints חנן; preserve surface variation.','5b:11','6a:1','6a:4')
add('rule','In that report, an alley breach at its side has the ten threshold.','6a:1')
add('rule','In that report, an alley breach at its front has the four threshold. Keep the thresholds distinct and preserve later corner qualification.','6a:1')
add('question','If the side breach counts as an entrance, why should the front breach not also count as an entrance?','6a:2')
add('qualification','Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua construes the front breach as occurring at a corner, where people do not ordinarily make an entrance. This answers the question rather than imposing the corner condition on every side breach.','6a:3')
add('opposing_position','Rav Huna says both side and front breaches use four. This contrasts with the report through Rav Hanin/Hanan, not with a similarly named son.','6a:4')
add('reported_support','Rav Huna tells Rav Hanan bar Rava not to dispute him because Rav acted in accordance with his view at Damharia. Treat the action as reported evidence, not a new direct speech by Rav.','6a:4')
add('answer','Rav Hanan answers that Rav found an open field and fenced it: the action was a situational protective restriction. It does not straightforwardly settle the general rule in Rav Huna’s favor.','6a:4')
add('attributed_support','Rav Nahman bar Yitzhak considers Rav Huna’s view plausible from the bent-alley dispute.','6a:5')
add('position','Rav treats a bent alley as through-open.','6a:5')
add('position','Shmuel treats a bent alley as closed. This is an explicitly attributed dispute, not an inference from nearby names.','6a:5')
add('question','The Gemara rejects setting the bent-alley dispute above ten, asking whether Shmuel could then call it closed.','6a:6')
add('inference','The setting is therefore taken at ten, and Rav’s through-open treatment is used to infer a side-breach threshold of four. This is the supporting argument’s inference, subsequently answered.','6a:7')
add('qualification_answer','Rav Hanan bar Rava distinguishes the bent alley because the public passes through there. Do not export that feature to every side breach.','6a:8')
add('inference_question','The Gemara infers Rav Huna applies his restriction even without public passage and asks how that differs from Rabbi Ammi/Rav Assi. This comparison requires earlier context and the following ridge distinction.','6a:9')
add('answer','The answer distinguishes residual raised remnants/ridges there from their absence here (גידודי). Preserve this physical qualifier, without replacing it with a different enclosure mechanism.','6a:10')
add('baraita_position','The first baraita view prescribes a doorway form on one side and the text’s lehi/beam remedy on the other. Literal דרך רה״ר framing must be qualified by6b4 as alleys opening to public domain.','6a:11','6b:4')
add('reported_position','Hanania reports Beit Shammai: a door on each side, and locking when going out and in. Separate door installation and locking requirement.','6a:11')
add('reported_position','Hanania reports Beit Hillel: a door on one side and the text’s lehi/beam remedy on the other. Do not automatically import Beit Shammai’s locking requirement;6b6–9 addresses this.','6a:11','6b:6','6b:9')
add('context_objection','The literal public-domain baraita framing is challenged by Rabbi Judah’s two-house case and the Sages’ objection that public domain cannot be joined that way.','6a:12','6b:1')
add('context_rule','Rabbi Judah’s two houses across public domain allow a lehi on each side or a beam on each side and carrying between; the replying Sages deny that method. Preserve alternatives and disagreement.','6b:1')
add('context_objection','The attempted answer that doors suffice is challenged by Rabbah bar bar Hana reporting Rabbi Yohanan: absent nighttime locking, Jerusalem would incur public-domain liability.','6b:2')
add('context_report','Ulla likewise says Mahuza gates would incur public-domain liability without locking. Do not conflate this actual public-domain case with the corrected alley case.','6b:3')
add('context_correction','Rav Yehuda corrects the baraita to alleys open to public domain, restating the first remedy. This controls interpretation of the final covered core.','6b:4')
add('context_position','Rav rules like the first tanna, whereas Shmuel rules like Hanania. Keep this remedy dispute distinct from their bent-alley classification dispute.','6b:5')
add('context_question','For Hanania according to Beit Hillel, must the door actually be locked?','6b:6')
add('context_transmission','Rav Yehuda reports Shmuel: no locking required; Rav Mattana also reports Shmuel the same. An alternate version presents Mattana’s own incident and Shmuel’s instruction; preserve versions rather than combining into one event.','6b:6')
add('context_report','Rav Anan points to Nehardea gates half buried in earth that Shmuel passed without comment, as evidence in the locking question.','6b:7')
add('context_qualification','Rav Kahana says those gates were מגופות (closed); preserve this qualification rather than assuming the earlier observation unopposed.','6b:8')
add('context_correction','Rav Nahman ordered the dirt removed; the inference that he requires locking is rejected. Doors need to be capable of locking even though not actually locked.','6b:9')
add('context_case','A Nehardea bent alley received doors, described initially as combining Rav’s through-open classification with Shmuel’s Hanania remedy. Each authority’s other ruling creates the mixed-stringency question.','6b:10','6b:11')
add('context_objection','A baraita condemns taking both schools’ leniencies and also contradictory combined stringencies, requiring consistency in a school’s lenient/strict sides. Its opening always-Beit-Hillel clause is itself questioned.','6b:12','6b:13')
add('context_alternatives','The baraita’s inconsistency is answered by before/after heavenly voice; alternatively both afterward according to Rabbi Yehoshua who disregards the voice; alternatively generalized consistent treatment of opposed tannaim/amoraim. Do not collapse these answers.','6b:14','6b:15','7a:1','7a:2')
add('context_answer','The alley problem remains. Rav Nahman bar Yitzhak says the practice was entirely Rav, using Rav Huna’s report of Rav: law so, but not publicly instructed. Preserve report-specific scope.','7a:3','7a:4')
add('context_objection','Rav Adda bar Ahava’s report of Rav that it is both law and taught is raised against that explanation.','7a:5')
add('context_distinction','Rav Shizvi distinguishes contradictory stringencies from noncontradictory stringencies, illustrating with spine/skull deficit measures and their impurity/terefah relation. This is an alternative reconciliation, not erasure of the earlier reports.','7a:6','7a:7','7a:8','7a:9')
add('context_exception','Rabbi Akiva’s doubled tithe practice on a first-Shevat etrog is challenged as contradictory stringencies; answer: he was uncertain whether Beit Hillel said1or15Shevat, so acted strictly under uncertainty. Do not classify this as choosing inconsistent schools.','7a:10','7a:11')
add('context_scope','Rav Yosef reports Rav Yehuda→Rav before Rav Huna: the dispute applies with streets on both ends or plazas on both ends. A street/field case is treated like field/field and gets the doorway-form plus lehi/beam remedy. Retain this later road-type limitation.','7a:12','7a:13','7a:14','7a:15')
